<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tuileries Palace</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1564">1564</a>–<a href="/years/1871">1871</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Palais des Tuileries</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tuileries Palace was a royal and imperial residence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, begun in 1564 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-de-medici">Catherine de' Medici</a> beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> was brought there from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> in 1789, and its storming on 10 August 1792 brought down the monarchy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> and later rulers lived there until 1870; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communards">Communards</a> set it on fire in May 1871, and the ruins were demolished in 1883, leaving the Tuileries Garden.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tuileries-palace.jpg" alt="Tuileries Palace" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gustave Le Gray · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_Palais_des_Tuileries_-_Le_Gray.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The site lay just outside the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, on land occupied by workshops that made tuiles, roof tiles. After the death of Henri II in 1559, his widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-de-medici">Catherine de' Medici</a> planned a new residence close to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> with space for a large garden. Between 1563 and 1568 she bought several plots, and construction began in 1564 with Philibert de l'Orme as chief architect. After he died in 1570 Jean Bullant took his place. The Day of the Barricades in 1588 halted the work.</p><p>Henri IV resumed construction in 1594 and built the Grande Galerie, which connected the Tuileries to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>. Between 1659 and 1661, and again from 1664 to 1666, Louis Le Vau enlarged the palace for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>. The finished façade was 266 metres long.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The court occupied the palace from 1667 to 1672 and then moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>. Louis XV lived there from 1716 to 1722. On 1 December 1783 the first manned flight in a hydrogen balloon, by Jacques Charles and the Robert brothers, took off from its garden. On 6 October 1789 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> and his family were forced to leave Versailles for the Tuileries, and the National Constituent Assembly later met in its Salle du Manège. After the failed flight to Varennes in June 1791 the royal family was returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and on 10 August 1792 a mob stormed the palace and massacred the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swiss-guards">Swiss Guards</a> who defended it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> then met there during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reign-of-terror">Reign of Terror</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> moved in on 19 February 1799 and had Charles Percier and Pierre Fontaine redesign the interior. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">House of Bourbon</a> returned after his fall, Louis Philippe d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> used the palace until 1848, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> took up residence in 1852 and extensively redecorated it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>On 23 May 1871, during the suppression of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a>, twelve men under Jules Bergeret set the palace on fire with petroleum, tar and turpentine. The fire lasted 48 hours and gutted it, apart from the foundations and the Arc de Triomphe du Carrousel. The walls stood for eleven years until the Third Republic had them demolished between February and 30 September 1883. Pieces of the stone were sold as souvenirs. The Tuileries Garden survives, as does the Axe historique running through the Place de la Concorde to the Arc de Triomphe, which was once centred on the palace façade.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tuileries Palace” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the insurrection of 10 August 1792 citizens stormed the <b>Tuileries Palace</b>, killed six hundred of the king's Swiss guards and demanded the king's removal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-first-republic">French First Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>War with Austria from April 1792 and fear of betrayal led to the storming of the <b>Tuileries</b> on 10 August 1792; the National Convention abolished the monarchy on 21 September 1792, and Louis XVI was guillotined in January 1793.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Swiss Guards died defending the <b>Tuileries</b> in Paris in August 1792, and revolutionary ideas found supporters in Vaud, Basel and among exiles such as Frédéric-César de La Harpe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the storming of the <b>Tuileries Palace</b> on the night of 9–10 August 1792, when the people of Paris forced the king to flee to the National Assembly and massacred his Swiss Guard, the crowd danced and sang the Carmagnole to celebrate.</blockquote><a href="/songs/carmagnole">La Carmagnole</a></li><li><blockquote>Government troops entered Paris on 21 May, and during the 'Bloody Week' that followed thousands of Communards were killed, many by summary execution; the Communards shot hostages and burned the <b>Tuileries</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a></li></ul></section>
